From: OCCTSwift ecosystem <noreply@secondmouse.au>
Subject: [PATCH] Application Framework - BinLDrivers reader refuses a label nested too deeply

BinLDrivers_DocumentRetrievalDriver::ReadSubTree reads a label, then calls itself once for each
child label. A file chooses how deep its labels nest, so it chooses how deep the call stack
goes: a binary OCAF file of 20000 nested labels, 320 KB, overflowed the 8 MB stack of the thread
that opened it (SIGSEGV), where 1000 levels load; on the 512 KB stack of a dispatch or Swift
concurrency worker, 1000 levels already overflow. Nothing in the file marks the depth, so a reader
that is handed the stream cannot know it ahead of the recursion.

The reader now counts the levels it is nested in on the calling thread and, past 512, reports
"labels nested too deeply" as a Message_Fail, sets PCDM_RS_UnrecognizedFileFormat and returns -1,
which every caller up the recursion already treats as a read failure. A document is nested far
less deeply than that. The counter is a function-local thread_local, so no header and no class layout changes, and a subclass that
overrides ReadSubTree and calls this one is counted too.

The bound is a constant: stack depth is a property of the thread. 512 levels take about 300 KB,
which a 512 KB worker holds; a thread with a smaller stack can still overflow below it.

Tracked as SecondMouseAU/OCCTSwift#3489, follow-up to #3486.

---
diff --git a/src/ApplicationFramework/TKBinL/BinLDrivers/BinLDrivers_DocumentRetrievalDriver.cxx b/src/ApplicationFramework/TKBinL/BinLDrivers/BinLDrivers_DocumentRetrievalDriver.cxx
index a6c2f9b33b..71545664cf 100644
--- a/src/ApplicationFramework/TKBinL/BinLDrivers/BinLDrivers_DocumentRetrievalDriver.cxx
+++ b/src/ApplicationFramework/TKBinL/BinLDrivers/BinLDrivers_DocumentRetrievalDriver.cxx
@@ -48,6 +48,29 @@
 #include <iostream>
 #include <limits>
 
+namespace
+{
+//! A label nested deeper than this is refused. ReadSubTree recurses once per level, about 600 bytes
+//! of stack each, and a file chooses its own depth: 20000 levels (a 320 KB file) overflowed an 8 MB
+//! stack, and 1000 levels overflow the 512 KB stack of a dispatch or Swift concurrency worker.
+//! 512 stays under what such a worker can hold and is far deeper than a document is nested.
+constexpr int THE_MAX_LABEL_DEPTH = 512;
+
+//! Counts the levels ReadSubTree is nested in on this thread.
+struct LabelDepthGuard
+{
+  LabelDepthGuard() { ++Depth(); }
+
+  ~LabelDepthGuard() { --Depth(); }
+
+  static int& Depth()
+  {
+    static thread_local int THE_DEPTH = 0;
+    return THE_DEPTH;
+  }
+};
+} // namespace
+
 IMPLEMENT_STANDARD_RTTIEXT(BinLDrivers_DocumentRetrievalDriver, PCDM_RetrievalDriver)
 
 #define SHAPESECTION_POS "SHAPE_SECTION_POS:"
@@ -686,6 +709,14 @@ int BinLDrivers_DocumentRetrievalDriver::ReadSubTree(
   int                        nbRead = 0;
   TCollection_ExtendedString aMethStr("BinLDrivers_DocumentRetrievalDriver: ");
 
+  const LabelDepthGuard aDepthGuard;
+  if (LabelDepthGuard::Depth() > THE_MAX_LABEL_DEPTH)
+  {
+    myMsgDriver->Send(aMethStr + "error: labels nested too deeply", Message_Fail);
+    myReaderStatus = PCDM_RS_UnrecognizedFileFormat;
+    return -1;
+  }
+
   Message_ProgressScope aPS(theRange, "Reading sub tree", 2, true);
 
   bool aSkipAttrs = false;
